
=== Rest Keys

Rest API keys must be set up in order to access the REST API. Attempting to access the REST API without a key will result in HTTP error code 659 (forbidden). When a new key is created, it will only be displayed once, so be sure to copy it and save it for future use.

The following is an example of how to include the key in a `curl` command:

[source, cli]
----
curl -X GET "http://localhost:31415/api/engine/corp-000/channelstatus" -H "accept: */*" -H "X-REST-API-KEY: gSrLaAEuKa3FLdUbuUZ1cRv7Mi2nVS"
----

A user interface for the REST API is available on the Help -> REST API screen. When the screen is first visited, the padlock icons are open to indicate that a REST API key is not being used. To enter the key, click the Authorize button, which is highlighted in red in the below image. After entering the key, the padlocks will close.

image::images/manage/rest-api.png[]

.Required Fields

[[key-name]]Key Name:: Name of application that will use this key.
[[key-description]]Key Description:: Additional description of the use.
[[access-level]]Access Level:: Level of access, either read-only or read-write.
.Levels
|===

|Readonly|Read-only users can only access the get methods. 

|Readwrite|Read-write users can access all methods, including post, put, and get.

|===

==== Default Rest Key

As an alternative to creating a key through the console, a default REST API key can be configured as a startup parameter. This is useful for recovering access to a remote node with an inaccessible web console, or for granting REST access automatically on a new or containerized install without any manual console setup.

Set the `rest.api.default.key` parameter (or the `SYM_REST_API_DEFAULT_KEY` environment variable) to a value that is at least 32 characters long. Shorter or blank values are ignored and have no effect, meaning the REST API will behave as if no default key were configured. This parameter only takes effect after the engine is deployed and started.

The default key grants the same full read-write access as a read-write console-managed key. Like any other REST API key, it only works when `rest.api.enable` (or `SYM_REST_API_ENABLE`) is set to `true`.

==== Authenticating REST Calls with a Bearer Token

When bearer token validation is turned on (See <<Bearer Token Authentication>>), a REST API call can be authenticated with a token instead of a REST API key. The token is sent in the `Authorization` header:

[source, cli]
----
curl -X GET "https://localhost:31415/api/engine/corp-000/channelstatus" -H "accept: */*" -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>"
----

A token gives read-only access and is accepted only for `GET` calls. The token is checked against the engine named in the URL. If the URL does not name an engine that exists, the first engine is used.

REST API keys keep working when validation is on. The call is authenticated as follows:

* A valid default key is accepted, and the token is not checked.
* A key that the node recognizes decides the call on its own, and the token is not checked. A `readwrite` key is accepted for any method. A `readonly` key is accepted for `GET` calls, and is rejected with HTTP status 659 (forbidden) for any other method, even if the call also has a valid token.
* A key that is not recognized, or no key at all, is accepted only if the call is a `GET` with a valid token.

The REST API must still be enabled with `rest.api.enable`. Unlike sync calls, REST calls are always checked, and `http.jwt.server.enforce.enabled` has no effect on them. A token for the REST API is not tied to a node, so the check of `http.jwt.server.node.id.claim` is not made.
